          %h2#idea Idea
          %h3#in_physics In physics
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            and especially in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum+mechanics"} continuum mechanics
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thermodynamics"} thermodynamics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physical+system"} physical system
            extended in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            is called
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em intensive
                if it is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
                on (the physical system extended in) space;
            %li
              %p
                %em extensive
                if it is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/density"} density
                or
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+distribution"} linear distribution
                on (the physical system extended in) space.
          %p
            For instance, for a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solid+body"} solid body
            its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/temperature"} temperature
            is intensive, but its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass"} mass
            is extensive: there is a temperature assigned to every point of the body (in the idealization of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum+mechanics"} continuum mechanics
            anyway) but a mass is assigned only to every little “extended” piece of the body, not to a single point.
          %p
            This terminology in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            appears vaguely in (
            %a{:href => "#Hegel1812"}> Hegel 1812
            ),
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Hegel1817"} Hegel 1817
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Grassmann1844"} Grassmann 1844
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Tolman"} Richard Tolman
            (
            %a{:href => "#Tolman1917"}> 1917
            ).
          %p If we take into account that a physical system may or may not have a particular kind of quantity (for example, a thermodynamic system may or may not have a temperature), a system has a value of an intensive quantity if and only if all of its subsystems have a value of that quantity, and then all of these values are equal. However, it’s generally safe to assume that every system has a value of every extensive quantity.
          %p
            To be precise, given a system
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_0
            made up of the subsystems
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_1
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_2
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                For an intensive quantity
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi q
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_0
                has a value
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi q
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q_0
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
                if and only if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_1
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_2
                also have values of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi q
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi q
                        %mn 1
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi q
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q_1 = q_0
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi q
                          %mn 2
                        %mo =
                        %msub
                          %mi q
                          %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q_2 = q_0
            %li
              %p
                For an extensive quantity
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Q
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Q
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Q
                          %mn 0
                        %mo =
                        %msub
                          %mi Q
                          %mn 1
                        %mo +
                        %msub
                          %mi Q
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q_0 = Q_1 + Q_2
          %h3#in_geometry_and_algebra In geometry and algebra
          %p
            In (
            %a{:href => "#Lawvere86"}> Lawvere 86
            ) it is amplified that this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality"} duality
            is generally a fundamental one also in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring+object"} ring object
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi CRing
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \in CRing(\mathbf{H})
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the space of
                %em intensive quantities
                on an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ∈
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \in \mathbf{H}
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+space"} mapping space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi R
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi H
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi CRing
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [X,R]_{\mathbf{H}} \in CRing(\mathbf{H})
                formed in
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi H
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            %li
              %p
                the space of
                %em extensive quantities
                on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                is the
                = succeed "-linear" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi R
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mo *
                      %mo ≔
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo ,
                      %mi R
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mrow
                          %mi R
                          %mi Mod
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [X,R]^\ast \coloneqq [[X,R], R]_{R Mod}
                formed in
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
                in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi H
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
              %p
                (here the operation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ,
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo ,
                      %mi R
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mrow
                          %mi R
                          %mi Mod
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [[-,R],R]_{R Mod}
                happens to be what is also called the “
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuation+monad"}> continuation monad
                ” for
                = succeed ")" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration"} integration
                map is the canonical
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/evaluation"} evaluation
                pairing
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∫
                        %mi X
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo ×
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi R
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mo *
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mi R
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\int_X \;\colon\; [X,R] \times [X,R]^\ast  \longrightarrow R
                      \\,.
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x, f) \mapsto f(x)
          %p
            See also at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+distribution"} Lawvere distribution
          %p The elusive connection between integration and (co)ends can here be explained:
          %p
            %strong Proposition:
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            be a monoidal closed category with tensor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes_V
            and internal hom
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [-, -]_V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mtext Lan
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mtext Id
                        %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mtext Id
                    %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{Lan}_{\text{Id}_V} (\text{Id}_V)
            exists and is pointwise. Then
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ≅
                  %msub
                    %mtext Id
                    %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %msub
                    %mtext Lan
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mtext Id
                        %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mtext Id
                    %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %msup
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi V
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi V
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \cong \text{Id}_V (X) \cong \text{Lan}_{\text{Id}_V} (\text{Id}_V)(X) \cong \int^{Y \in V} [Y, X]_V \otimes_V Y
          %p and the structure maps are
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mi Y
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi V
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi V
                  %mi Y
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon_Y : [Y, X]_V \otimes_V Y \rightarrow X
          %p Occuring as counits in the supposed monoidal closed adjunction.
          %p
            This is an instance of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/Kan+extension#PointwiseByCoEnds"} coend formula for a Kan extension
          %p
            We may think of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            as the category of
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            resembling spaces. In that case, we get:
          %p
            %strong Corollary:
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            be a category with internal hom
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [-, -]_{\mathbf{H}}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            be a commutative ring object in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mtext -mod
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \text{-mod}
            be the category of
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes_R
            (tensor product) and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [-, -]_R
            (internal Hom). Then, taking
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo =
                  %mi R
                  %mtext -mod
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V = R \text{-mod}
            above, and supposing that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mtext Lan
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mtext Id
                        %mrow
                          %mi R
                          %mtext -mod
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mtext Id
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                      %mtext -mod
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{Lan}_{\text{Id}_{R \text{-mod}}} (\text{Id}_{R \text{-mod}})
            we have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo ≅
                  %msup
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi R
                      %mtext -mod
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi R
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi R
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi R
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \cong  \int^{X \in R \text{-mod}} [X, R]_R  \otimes_R X
          %p with structure maps:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi R
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi R
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi R
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon_X : [X, R]_R \otimes_R X \rightarrow R
          %p
            In the above notation,
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϵ
                      %mi X
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon_X = \int_X
          %p
            %strong Example:
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            be the category of
            %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/metric+space#LawvereMetricSpace"} Lawvere metric spaces
            (Note: this category is not a topos, but the corollary above will still apply). Maps are short maps.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            has an internal Hom consisting of bounded maps. Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            be the real numbers, with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi x
                  %mo −
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d(x, y) = ||x - y||
            as Lawvere metric.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mtext -mod
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R} \text{-mod}
            is here the category of normed
            = succeed "-modules," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ||x|| = 0
            does not imply that
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x = 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo =
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ||x|| = \infty
            is possible. (This temporary notation is not to be confused with the category of real vector spaces over the real numbers, in which norms play no role).
          %p
            There is a natural notion of
            %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/Cauchy+complete+category"} completion
            on such spaces. The category of complete objects is essentially the category of Banach spaces over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ||x|| = 0
            does not imply that
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x = 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo =
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ||x|| = \infty
            is possible. Completion makes this category into a reflective subcategory of
            = succeed "-mod." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mtext LawvBan
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{LawvBan}
            for the this category - it is a natural continuation of Lawvere metric spaces.
          %p
            There is a natural choice of monoidal closed structure on
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mtext LawvBan
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{LawvBan}
            %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+Banach+spaces"} projective tensor product
            (note: slight tweaking is necessary to account for the differences between Lawvere Banach spaces and Banach spaces). The internal Hom consists of bounded maps. The corollary above applies to this setup, so that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo ≅
                  %msup
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi R
                      %mtext -mod
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi R
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi R
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi R
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \cong  \int^{X \in R \text{-mod}} [X, R]_R  \otimes_R X
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes_R
            is projective tensor product and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi R
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [X, R]_R
            consists of bounded maps from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi R
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi R
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi R
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon_X : [X, R]_R \otimes_R X \rightarrow R
          %p
            sending
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \otimes x
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi R
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi R
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi R
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_X : [X, R]_R \otimes_R X \rightarrow R
          %h3#in_higher_geometry_and_higher_algebra In higher geometry and higher algebra
          %p
            Viewed this way, this naturally generalizes to the case where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            is in fact an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi CRing
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \in CRing(\mathbf{H})
            an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+ring"} E-∞ ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [X,R]
            is called the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectrum
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi R
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [X,R]^\ast
            is the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+homology"} generalized homology
            spectrum. In this form intensive and extensive properties appear in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+quantization"} motivic quantization
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+prequantum+field+theory"} local prequantum field theory
          %p
            More generally, for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi χ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi
            an
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-line+bundle"} (∞,1)-line bundle
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            then the corresponding extensive object is the
            = succeed "-twisted" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi χ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thom+spectrum"} Thom spectrum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mrow
                      %mo •
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                      %mi χ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_{\bullet + \chi}(X)
            and the intensive object is the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi χ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+cohomology"} twisted cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectrum
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi R
                      %mrow
                        %mo •
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                        %mi χ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mrow
                        %mo •
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                        %mi χ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi R
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                        %mi Mod
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R^{\bullet + \chi}(X) = [R_{\bullet+ \chi}(X),R]_{R Mod}
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+quantization"} motivic quantization
            for how this appears in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
          %h2#in_modal_homotopy_type_theory In modal homotopy type theory
          %p
            Assume we are working in the context of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} cohesive (∞,1)-topos
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+modalities"} adjoint modalities
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+modality"} shape modality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊣
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dashv
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+modality"} flat modality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊣
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dashv
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sharp+modality"} sharp modality
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∫
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mo ♭
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mo ♯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int \dashv \flat \dashv \sharp
          %p
            We may characterize the codomains of those
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
            which are
            %em intensive
            or
            %em extensive quantities
            in terms of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ♯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sharp
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %strong> Intensive
                \: functions whose value is genuinely given by their restriction to all possible points have as codomains types
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                that are fully determined by their moment of continuity, that is those for which
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mo ♯
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to \sharp X
                is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism"} monomorphism
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
                these are the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+objects"} concrete objects
                or equivalently the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separated+presheaves"} separated presheaves
                for
                = succeed ":" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ♯
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sharp
            %li
              %p
                %strong> Extensive
                \: objects which have purely the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/negative+moment"} negative moment
                of continuity
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mo ♯
                          %mo ¯
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{\sharp}
                %em extend
                beyond a single point. For example, the smooth moduli space of differential
                = succeed "-forms" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                %em extension
                is precisely that which gave the name to
                = succeed "\u2018s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hermann+Grassmann"} Hermann Grassmann
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ausdehnungslehre"} Ausdehnungslehre
                that introduced the concept of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exterior+algebra"} exterior
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} differential form
          %p
            So, the adjunction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ♭
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mo ♯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\flat \dashv \sharp)
            expresses
            = succeed "," do
              %em quantity
          %h2#references References
          %p The concepts of intensive and extensive quantity are highlighted in
          %ul
            %li#Grassmann1814
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hermann+Grassmann"}> Hermann Grassmann
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ausdehnungslehre"} Ausdehnungslehre
          %p
            which states (p. xxiv, xxv) that intensive quantity is the topic of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+calculus"} differential calculus
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration"} integration
            theory, while extensive quantity is the topic of this very
            = succeed "." do
              %em Ausdehnungslehre
          %p General discussion includes
          %ul
            %li
              Wikipedia,
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intensive_and_extensive_properties"} Intensive and extensive properties
          %p
            A formalization in
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+logic"} categorical logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
            is proposed in
          %ul
            %li#Lawvere86
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              , Introduction to
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Categories+in+Continuum+Physics"}> Categories in Continuum Physics
                , Lectures given at a Workshop held at SUNY, Buffalo 1982. Lecture Notes in Mathematics 1174. 1986
          %p
            See also the exposition of his ideas at
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "Higher+toposes+of+laws+of+motion#iii_extensiveintensive_duality_and_cohomological_quantization"} Higher toposes of laws of motion
          %p
            Lawvere’s terminology is probably (see at
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objective+logic"} objective logic
          %ul
            %li#Hegel1812
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Hegel"}> Georg Hegel
                , section
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a{:href => "Science+of+Logic#ExtensiveAndIntensiveQuantity"} Extensives und Intensives Quantum
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Science+of+Logic"} Science of Logic
            %li#Hegel1817
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Hegel"}> Georg Hegel
                , part II “Philosophy of Nature”, second section “Physics”, B,
                %a{:href => "Science+of+Logic#PN298b"} §298b
                in
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Encyclopedia+of+the+Philosophical+Sciences"} Encyclopedia of the Philosophical Sciences
          %p
            and meant to be a formalization of this part of the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objective+logic"}> objective logic
            ”, see also at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Science+of+Logic"} Science of Logic
          %p The first use of the terms ‘intensive’ and ‘extensive’ appears to be
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Tolman"} Richard Tolman
              (1917). The Measurable Quantities of Physics. Physical Review 9 (3): 237–253.
        .revisedby
